          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Model structures on chain complexes are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structures on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+chain+complexes"} categories of chain complexes
            whose weak equivalences are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} Hurewicz model structure on chain complexes
            whose weak equivalences are
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopy+equivalences"} chain homotopy equivalences
          %p
            Via these model structures, all of the standard techniques in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolutions"} projective resolutions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+resolutions"} cofibrant resolutions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolutions"} fibrant resolutions
          %p The existence of these model structures depends subtly on whether the chain complexes in question are bounded or not.
          %h3#in_nonnegative_degree In non-negative degree
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} Chain complexes
            in non-negative degree in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are special in that they may be identified via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ch_{\bullet \geq 0}(A) \simeq A^{\Delta^{op}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complexes
            are identified with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+object"} cosimplicial object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ch^{\bullet \geq 0}(A) \simeq A^{\Delta}
                  \\,.
          %p
            At least if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{\Delta^{op}}
            is the category of abelian
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presents
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %p
            The model structure on chain complexes transports this presentation of the special
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Analogous statements apply to the category of unbounded chain complexes and the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (infinity,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec"} Spec
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
          %p This we discuss below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#CochainNonNeg"} In non-negative degree
          %h3#for_unbounded_chain_complexes For unbounded chain complexes
          %p
            Model structures on unbounded (co)chain complexes can be understood as presentations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+objects"} spectrum objects
            in model structures of bounded (co)chain complexes.
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ForUnboundedChainComplexes"} For unbounded chain complexes
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#CochainNonNeg In non-negative degree
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
          %p
            Recall that by the dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\Delta
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+object"} cosimplicial object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equivalent to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^\bullet_+(C)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complexes"} cochain complexes
            in non-negative degree. This means that we can transfer results discussed at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cosimplicial+objects"} model structure on cosimplicial objects
            to cochain complexes (see
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0312/0312531v1.pdf"} Bousfield2003, section 4.4
            for more).
          %h4#StandardQuillenOnBounded The standard (Quillen) model structures
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \coloneqq R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
          %p We discuss the
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ProjectiveStructureOnChainComplexes"} Projective structure on chain complexes
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#InjectiveStructureOnCochainComplexes"} Injective structure on cochain complexes
          %h5#ProjectiveStructureOnChainComplexes Projective structure on chain complexes
          #ProjectiveModelStructure.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              structure on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_{\bullet \geq 0 }(\mathcal{A})
              (in non-negative degree) whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
                  are the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
                  are the morphisms that are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
                  in each
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
                  degree;
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
                  are degreewise
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
                  with degreewise
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            %p
              called the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong projective model structure
          %p
            The projective model structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_{\bullet \geq 0}
            is originally due to (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen67"}> Quillen 67, II.4, pages II.4.11, II.4.12
            ). See also (
            %a{:href => "#GoerssSchemmerhorn06"}> Goerss-Schemmerhorn 06, Theorem 1.5
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Dungan10"} Dungan 10, 2.4.2, proof in section 2.5
          %p
          #ProjectiveModelStructureOnConnectiveChainComplexesIsProper.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The projective model structure on connective chain complexes is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} proper model category
          (e.g.
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "#Jardine03"} Jardine 03, Prop. 1.5
          %p
          #ProjectiveModelStructureOnConnectiveChainComplexesIsMonoidal.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              With respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
              this is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+model+category"} monoidal model category
          (e.g.
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Jardine03"} Jardine 03, Prop. 1.5
          = succeed ")." do
            %a{:href => "#SchwedeShipley03"} Schwede &amp; Shipley 2003, p. 312 (26 of 48)
          %h5#InjectiveStructureOnCochainComplexes Injective structure on cochain complexes
          %p Dually
          #InjectiveModelStructure.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              structure on non-negatively graded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complexes"} cochain complexes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\bullet \geq 0 }(\mathcal{A})
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
              %li
                %p
                  cofibrations are the morphisms that are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
                  in each
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
                  degree;
              %li
                %p
                  fibrations are degreewise
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
                  with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            %p
              called the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong injective model structure
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Dungan10"}> Dungan 10, Theorem 2.4.5
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This means that a chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet \in Ch_{\bullet}(\mathcal{A})
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+object"} cofibrant object
              in the projective model structure, theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProjectiveModelStructure"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+modules"} projective modules
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+resolution"} cofibrant resolution
              in the projective model structure is precisely what in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
              is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolution"} projective resolution
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolutions"} fibrant resolutions
              in the injective model structure, theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InjectiveModelStructure"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
              in homological algebra.
            %p
              This way the traditional definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor+in+homological+algebra"} derived functor in homological algebra
              relates to the general construction of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              theory. See there for more details. Similar comments apply to the various other model structures below.
          %h4#GeneralResults Resolution model structures
          %p
            There are
            %em resolution model structures
            on cosimplicial objects in a model category, due to (
            %a{:href => "#DwyerKanStover"}> DwyerKanStover
            ), reviewed in (
            %a{:href => "#Bousfield"}> Bousfield
            )
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p (…)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Obj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \in Obj(A)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              of objects, such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              has enough
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
            %p
              Then there is a model category structure on non-negatively graded cochain complexes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\bullet \geq 0}(A)_{\mathcal{G}}
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                  such that for each
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \in A
                  the induced map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(Y,K) \to A(X,K)
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
                  of chain complexes of abelian groups;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is a cofibration if it is
                  = succeed "-monic" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒢
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is a fibration if it is degreewise a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
                  with degreewise
                  = succeed "-injective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒢
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0312/0312531v1.pdf"} Bousfield2003, section 4.4
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has enough
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            is the class of all of them, this reproduces the
            %a{:href => "#StandardQuillenOnBounded"} standard Quillen model structure
            discussed above:
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              with enough
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\geq 0}(A)
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are the
                  = succeed "s;" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  fibrations are the morphisms that are
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                  in each degree;
              %li
                %p
                  cofibrations are the morphisms which are
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  in each
                  %em positive
                  degree.
          %p
            If we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            to be the class of all objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            this gives the following structure.
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              There is a model structure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi tot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\bullet\geq 0}(A)_{tot}
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are cochain
                  = succeed "s;" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              %li
                %p
                  fibrations are the morphisms that are degreewise
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
                  = succeed "s;" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
              %li
                %p
                  cofibration are the morphisms that are in positive degree
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              is a category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              over some
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              is a homotopy equivalence. Moreover, in this case every chain complex is quasi-isomorphic to its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
              (regarded as a chain complex with zero differentials).
            %p
              This is the model structure which induces the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transferred+model+structure"} transferred
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-algebras
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              that is used in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
          %h4#CochainNonNegProj Projective model structure on connective cochain complexes
          %p
            We discuss a model structure on connective cochain complexes of abelian groups in which the fibrations are the degreewise epis. This follows an analogous proof in (
            %a{:href => "model+structure+on+dg-algebras#Jardine97"}> Jardine 97
            ).
          #ProjectiveModelStructureOnConnectiveCochainComplexes.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (projective model structure on connective cochain complexs )
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\bullet \geq 0}(Ab)
              of non-negatively graded cochain complexes of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
              becomes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              with
            %ul
              %li
                %p fibrations the degreewise surjections;
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences the
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            %p
              Moreover this is a
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
              induced from the dual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo +
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Ab
                      %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^\bullet_+(Ab) \simeq Ab^\Delta
              by the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ab
                      %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab^\Delta
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cosimplicial+objects"} category of cosimplicial objects
              (see there) in a category with all limits and colimits.
          %p
            The first part of this theorem is claimed, without proof, in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CastiglioniCortinas03"} Castiglioni-Cortinas 03, Def. 4.7
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We spell out a proof of the model structure below in a sequence of lemmas. The proof that this is a simplicial model category is at
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cosimplicial+abelian+groups"} model structure on cosimplicial abelian groups
          %p
            We record a detailed proof of the model structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^{\bullet \geq 0}(Ab)
            with fibrations the degreewise surjections, following the appendix of (
            %a{:href => "#Stel"}> Stel 10
            ).
          %p
            As usual, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n]
            for the complex concentrated on the additive group of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]
            for the cochain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi Id
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0 \to \cdots 0 \to \mathbb{Z} \stackrel{Id}{\to} \mathbb{Z} \to 0 \cdots)
            with the two copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            let
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[-1,0] = 0
          #ProjStructGenCofibs.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              the canonical maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n] \to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]
              are cofibrations, in that they have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
              against acyclic fibrations.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : A \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} B
              be degreewise surjective and an isomorphism on cohomology.
            %p
              First consider
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[0]\to \mathbb{Z}[-1,0] = 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi σ
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{Z}[0] &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^{\mathllap{\sigma}}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{p}
                    \\\
                    0 &amp;\stackrel{}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(f_0(1)) = 0
              we have by using that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a quasi-iso that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi mod
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi im
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0(1) = 0 \; mod\; im d_A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0(1) = 0
            %p
              Consider now
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n] \to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi σ
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n] &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^{\mathllap{\sigma}}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{p}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n-1,n] &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Such a lift is equivalently an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in A_{n-1}
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi A
                        %mi σ
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A \sigma = f_n(1)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{n-1}(\sigma) = g_{n-1}(1)
                  \.
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a quasi-isomorphism, and since it takes the closed element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n(1) \in A_n
              to the exact element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_n(f_n(1)) = d_B g_{n-1}(1)
              it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n(1)
              itself must be exact in that there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in A_{n-1}
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi A
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A z = f_n(1)
            %p
              So then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_B ( p(z) - g_{n-1}(1) ) = 0
              and again using that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a quasi-isomorphism this means that there must be a closed
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A_{n-1}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi B
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(a) = p(z)- g_{n-1}(1) + d_B b
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B_{n-2}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is degreewise onto, there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(a') = b
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi A
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(a) = p(z) - g_{n-1}(1) + p(d_A a')
            %p Set then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                    %mo −
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi A
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma := z - a + d_A a'
                    \\,.
            %p
              It follows with the above that this satisfies the two conditions on
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi σ
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi z
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi a
                            %mo +
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi a
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi f
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    d_A \sigma &amp;= d_A z - d_A a + d_A d_A a'
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    d_A z
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = f_n(1)
                    \\end{aligned}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi σ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo +
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi A
                            %mi a
                            %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi n
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    p( \sigma ) &amp;=  p(z) - p(a) + p(d_A a')
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = g_{(n-1)}(1)
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Finally consider
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n]
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi σ
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    0 &amp;\stackrel{}{\to}&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^{\mathllap{\sigma}}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{p}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n] &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Such a lift is a choice of element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in A_n
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi A
                        %mi σ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A \sigma = 0
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\sigma) = g_n(1)
                  ;
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n(1)
              is closed and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              a surjective quasi-isomorphism, we may find a closed
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A_n
              and an
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \in A_{n-1}
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p (a) = g_{n}(1) + d_B(p(a'))
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi A
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma := a - d_A a'
                    \\,.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              For all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]
              are acyclic cofibrations, in that they have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
              again all degreewise surjections.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              this is trivial. For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
              a diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    0 &amp;\to&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{p}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n-1,n] &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
            %p
              is equivalently just any element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{n-1}(1) \in B
              and a lift
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              accordingly just any element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in A
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\sigma) = g_{n-1}(1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is degreewise surjctive by assumption.
          #ProjStructCharAcyclicFibrations.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              is an acyclic fibration precisely if it has the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
              against
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n] \to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              By the above lemmas, it remains to show only one direction: if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              has the RLP, then it is an acyclic fibration.
            %p
              So assume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              has the RLP. Then from the existence of the lifts
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    0 &amp;\to&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n] &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
            %p
              one deduces that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is degreewise surjective on closed elements. In particular this means it is surjective in cohomology.
            %p With that, it follows from the existence of all the lifts
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi σ
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n] &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^{\mathllap{\sigma}}\nearrow&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n-1,n] &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              a lift of the closed element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n(1)
              that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is degreewise surjective on all elements.
            %p
              Moreover, these lifts say that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n(1)
              is any closed element such that under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              it becomes exact (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_B g_{n-1}(1) = p(f_n(1))
              ), then it must already be exact itself (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A \sigma_{n-1}(1) = f_n(1)
              ). Hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is also injective on cohomology and hence by the above is an isomorphism on cohomology.
          #ProjStrucFactAxiomI.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_4 Lemma
            %p
              Every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              can be factored as a morphism with left lifting property against all fibrations followed by a fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              Apply the
              = succeed "-reasoning" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J = \{0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]\}
            %p
              Since for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[n,n+1]\to B
              corresponds to an element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B_n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi n
                                    %mo ∈
                                    %mi 𝕟
                                %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi b
                                    %mo ∈
                                    %msub
                                      %mi B
                                      %mi n
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    0 &amp;\to&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    \\coprod_{{n \in \mathbb{n}} \atop {b \in B_n}}
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n,n+1] &amp;\stackrel{}{\to}&amp;
                    B
                    }
            %p
              one obtains a factorization through its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi j
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi n
                                    %mo ∈
                                    %mi 𝕟
                                %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi b
                                    %mo ∈
                                    %msub
                                      %mi B
                                      %mi n
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi p
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \&amp;{}^{\mathllap{j}}\swarrow&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    A \coprod \coprod_{{n \in \mathbb{n}} \atop {b \in B_n}}
                    \\mathbb{Z}[n,n+1]
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;\searrow_{p}&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              is the pushout of an acyclic cofibration, it is itself an acyclic cofibration. Moreover, since the cohomology of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi 𝕟
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi b
                            %mo ∈
                            %msub
                              %mi B
                              %mi n
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_{{n \in \mathbb{n}} \atop {b \in B_n}} \mathbb{Z}[n,n+1]
              clearly vanishes, it is a quasi-isomorphism.
            %p
              The map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is manifestly degreewise onto and hence a fibration.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_5 Lemma
            %p
              Every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              may be factored as a cofibration followed by an acyclic fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              By a
              %a{:href => "#ProjStructCharAcyclicFibrations"} lemma above
              acyclic fibrations are precisely the maps with the right lifting property against morphisms in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = \{0 \to \mathbb{Z}[n], \mathbb{Z}[n]\to \mathbb{Z}[n-1,n]\}
              %a{:href => "#ProjStructGenCofibs"} first lemma above
              are cofibrations.
            %p
              The claim then follows again from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
              apllied to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_6 Lemma
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              that is both a cofibration (:= LLP against acyclic fibrations ) and a weak equivalence has the left lifting property against all fibrations.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p
              By a standard argument, this follows from the factorization lemma proven
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#ProjStrucFactAxiomI"} above
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp;\stackrel{j}{\to}&amp; \hat B
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{f}}\searrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{p}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; B
                    }
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
